ROTINC
++++++

SUMMARY
-------

Rotate a set of 3 perpendicular components either around the vertical direction
(TO VRT or TO VNE) or around the T direction (TO LQT)

SYNTAX FOR ROTATIONS AROUND THE VERTICAL
----------------------------------------

  ``ROTINC TO [VRT|VNE] {VERBOSE}``

SYNTAX FOR ROTATIONS AROUND THE TRANSVERSE DIRECTION
----------------------------------------------------

  ``ROTINC TO [LQT] {INCIDENCE i | iP | iS} {VP alpha} {VS beta} {RAY{DEG|KM} par}  {FREESURFACE} {VERBOSE}``

INPUT
-----
      :TO VRT: rotate into vertical,radial, transverse coordinate system

      :TO VNE: rotate into system aligned with vertical, N, and E

      :TO LQT: rotate into P, SV, and SH coordinate system.  cmpinc for L is the apparent angle

      :iP: apparent angle for P incidence, computed from Vp,Vs and Ray Parameter

      :iS: apparent angle for S incidence, computed from Vp,Vs and Ray Parameter

      :VP v: P wave velocity near surface (default 5.8)

      :VS v: S wave velocity near surface (default 3.36)

      :RAYKM v: ray parameter = horizontal slowness, sin(theta)/v

      :RAYDEG v: ray parameter = horizontal slowness, r*sin(theta)/v

      :FREESURFACE: use free surface response instead of propagation vector

      :VERBOSE: prints information to the screen (default off)

DESCRIPTION
-----------

  Given three perpendicular components of a recorded time series, rotinc
  rotates the set into the selected coordinate system. It works its way
  through all records in memory until it fails to find 3 consecutive
  perpendicular traces. It assumes vertical up (cmpinc = 0.0), cmpaz
  measured clockwise from N.  T to the right from the vertical-radial
  plane (baz - 90).  Hence,  VNE, VRT, and LQT are all left-handed coordinate
  systems.  For LQT with FREESURFACE, cmpinc in L is the apparent angle: the
  arctangent of the radial (R) amplitude  over the vertical (V) amplitude.

EXAMPLES
--------

  Rotations around the vertical are straightforward, but there are several options
  for TO LQT.  (Because additional arguments are required, TO LQT is not explicitly
  required)::

    SAC> rotinc iP raykm 0.063 freesurface verbose
      Target Coordinate System: LQT
      Apparent Angle Method: 'Vp Free Surface'
      Vp: 5.80 km/s Vs: 3.36 km/s Ray Param: 0.063000 s/km
      Apparent angle: 24.44

  For this case, the V-R paticle motion gave an angle more than 10 degrees higher
  for the apparent angle.  The pulse width is about 20 seconds, so the arrivals
  average over the whole crut.  The following run gives more consistency::

    SAC> rotinc iP vp 8.04 vs 4.47 raykm 0.063 freesurface verbose
      Target Coordinate System: LQT
      Apparent Angle Method: 'Vp Free Surface'
      Vp: 8.04 km/s Vs: 4.47 km/s Ray Param: 0.063000 s/km
      Apparent angle: 32.71

INCIDENCE
---------

  Apparent, or incidence, angles are computed using the following equations:

  * Vp, No Free Surface, Whole Space

    angle = arcsin(Vp * p)

  * Vs, No Free Surface, Whole Space

    angle = arcsin(Vs * p)

  * Vp at Free Surface

    angle = arctan( 2 Vs p sqrt(1 - Vs**2 p**2) / (1 - 2 Vs**2 p**2) )

  * Vs at Free Surface

    angle = 90 - arctan( Vp * (1 - 2 Vs**2 p**2) / (2 Vs**2 p sqrt(||1 - Vp**2 p**2||)) )

  At the Free Surface, the angle of the P wave is dependent on the S-wave only and the
  angle of the S wave is dependent on both the P wave and S wave.  Equations are based
  on Problem 5.6 in Aki and Richards, 2002, pg. 184.

HEADER CHANGES
--------------

  CMPINC, CMPAZ, KCMPNM, DEPMAX, DEPMIN, DEPMEN

ERROR MESSAGES
--------------
- 1002: Bad value for
- 1011: Bad command file syntax; must specify an apparent-angle method
- 1301: No data file read in.
- 1304: Illegal operation on data file
- 1336: Undefined header field value.

